ople who  say we don't need to worry about this  and I'm not talking about those who say  God would never have set up a planet  that we could damage right I'm not  talking about that view I'm talking very  specifically about the view which is I'm  not worried about this because humans  are smart and resourceful and will  always stay slightly ahead of it  actually becoming a crisis how do you  communicate with someone like that and  explain to them that the crisis is now  that we're seeing more you know heat  related deaths among the very young and  elder  than we've seen for a long time whether  of that whatever argument you can make  how can you explain that this is already  sort of outpacing human ingenuity well I  think that what people really need to  appreciate with climate change and why  climate scientists have really been  trying desperately to issue these  warnings is that there's a tremendous  amount of inertia in the climate system  and it's not the case that you can say  and the nursery springs from a few  different directions as it were first of  all they're still warming in the  pipeline so even if we just really  stopped emitting co2 basically tomorrow  which you know evidently we're not going  to do there's still a lot of warming  left in the system until the climate  reaches a new equilibrium and I'm not  going to go into the kind of geophysics  of that but it's pretty basic science um  and so what you're seeing what you see  out your window the idea that okay when  it gets really bad we'll fix it  that is simply in this case  unfortunately not possible because  you've already set things in motion that  you can't get back this is why  scientists are so worried for example is  the Antarctic West Antarctic ice sheets  starting to disintegrate because once  you set that in motion there's a lot of  inertia in that system and you're not  going to get that back  so that's one reason why we really need  to be proactive as opposed to reactive  another reason is obviously just that  energy systems take a long time to  convert you know it's very very  difficult to our whole economy the  entire world economy of basically for  all intents and purposes right now runs  on fossil fuels if you want to change  that which evidently we have to you've  got to get going and you've got to get  started and you can't expect it to  happen tomorrow so there's a lot of  inertia in that system as well and when  you sort of you know add together all  these forms of inertia you realize that  we really needed to start this process a  long long time ago is there anything  you're seeing that makes you at all  optimistic and it could be either at the  you know micro level at a more macro  level it could be you know  Changez we're seeing in China or India  that are slowly moving away from fossil  fuels as the soul sort of provider of  energy even though it's happening  relatively slowly something that you  know a private company like Tesla is  doing we've had seven or eight countries  in Europe that have said over the next  10 or 20 years they want to get all  fossil fuel using vehicles off the roads  I mean it all sort of feels like they're  not really the strongest commitment they  could be changed if it ends up not being  that convenient and even still it's it  might end up being sort of too little  too late I mean is there something to be  optimistic about well I think that those  are the wrong you know those are sort of  the wrong categories that we keep  looking for some I think one of the  other problems that we have in dealing  with climate changes that we keep  looking for some answer there's going to  be one answer that's going to sort of  silver bullet and it's just not that  kind of a problem you're going to need a  million different answers but I do think  the one hopeful development I suppose  you could say is you know the really  really radical decline in the price of  solar power so that there are now times  of day in certain parts of the country  certain parts of the world where you  know basically are giving away power and  once that happens there's a lot of  things you know that you can do with  that power that might be really useful  like you know charge your electric car  and things like that so I think that  that is a very positive development and  one thing that it's show is if you talk  to it experts is that you know that  didn't just happen  that happened because there were a lot  of entities including you know the  Chinese government and the German  government which put a lot of money into  solar power and what's known as feed-in  tariffs where you basically utilities  had to buy solar power all these things  you know that I won't get into the too  bogged down in the bureaucratic details  but a lot of lot of effort on the part  of a lot of Governors governments and  also entrepreneurs went into this and we  need that on many many different fronts  so that unfortunately brings us back to  the current administration which is  actually you know  the budget just slashed R&amp;D on a lot of  these programs which is obviously  exactly a long way to go yeah I mean  along those lines at face value at the  30,000 foot level it seems well one  thing that should happen is government  should stop subsidizing oil in the  production of gasoline and instead put  those subsidies towards renewable energy  is it that simple would that make a big  difference that would make a huge  difference I think people don't really  appreciate you know how many tax once  again we don't we don't see them unless  you're in the know oil industry the coal  industry you don't know where the tax  breaks are but they're huge and people  have compiled them and those amounts to  government subsidies and when you  compile them they're in that you know I  can't remember the you know hundreds of  billions of dollars if their I don't  remember what the figures are but  they're very very high and if we took  all that you know that pot of money  which is basically a pot of missing  revenues and we put it toward you know  you took it away from things that we  don't want and put it towards things  that we do want I don't want to say it  would solve the problem but it would go  it would be a huge step in the right  direction let's put it that way  we've been speaking with Elizabeth  Kolbert she writes for The New Yorker  also author of the book the sixth  extinction which I recommend highly to  the audience Elizabeth thank you so much  for talking to us about this thanks for  having me
 

